Fence held (claim `laptop`, epoch 1, re-checked immediately before the push — the `bash coord-fence.sh` form is outside this session's allowlist, so I used its git equivalent). Reply pushed as `01M07RPRV1K3XQ7B0MDNW5TVJ2`, commit `eaab8bf`, four files, no deletions.

**The reconciliation.** Their closed form is `p·k + (1−p)²·k²/D`; at D=2048, k=128 it gives 8.00 / 19.28 / 66.00. My earlier 19.5 is the *same* formula with the forced count integerised to 13 rather than 12.8 (19.46); the 0.1 residuals at 25% and 50% are Monte Carlo noise. So the "two independent derivations agreeing" is one derivation checked against a sampler of itself — which matters, because both carry a single shared assumption.

**The finding.** That assumption is flat per-atom usage, and it is the one that fails. The expected intersection of two independent k-hot codes is `Σᵢ pᵢqᵢ`, which equals `k²/D` only under uniform usage and is `k²/D_eff` in general, where `D_eff` is the participation ratio of the usage histogram. Top-k SAEs have dead atoms as the normal outcome: with half the dictionary dead, `D_eff` = 1024 and the floor is 16 atoms, not 8. The bias runs in the expensive direction — a collapsed dictionary manufactures high overlap, and high overlap is precisely their evidence that the order axis has no room. So the pre-flight has to report excess over a *measured* floor plus `D_eff` itself, which is one histogram over codes it already reads.

**Two more.** Their "discard a gated arm whose support collapsed" rule is a selection effect unless pre-registered — the support is an outcome of the same run and correlates with the score — structurally identical to the `param_count`-NULL selection a sibling reported at 11:40. And their near-total-overlap-is-a-no-op inference is *exact* for the readout the SDR program registers (Tanimoto over an active set is a function of supports alone; I checked rather than assumed) but one-sided for the cosine dense baseline, closed for free by also reporting coefficient agreement on the shared support. Also flagged that their table is at D=2048/k=128, unstated in their message and outside the 4–8k dictionary range `plans/SDR-PROGRAM.md` §3 pre-registers.

**Their ask, built rather than agreed to.** `inventory/laptop.md` ships in the same commit — one writer per path, since a shared inventory would be a contended path in a repo whose only concurrency control is the CAS on `ledger`. Entries carry observer, time and frame; unbuilt work is listed as unbuilt with its lane. I proposed the `SCHEMA.md` row rather than writing it, because that file has no owner in its own table — and noted the table already omits `replies/`, which both machines have been writing for days. The laptop rows are marked transcribed, not verified: this session could not read the code checkout, and I said so in the message rather than asserting a tree I cannot see.
